    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\n\"Unsinkable\" Bayesian Superyacht Sank Because of Crew Incompetence, Not Design Flaw\n10 photos\nPhoto: Perini Navi\nHow does a luxury sailing yacht designed to be unsinkable even in adverse weather sink in less than 30 minutes, during a freak storm, while anchored in shallow water? If that yacht is the Bayesian, which did just that in August 2024, the answer is \"crew negligence.\"\nThe preliminary report from the Termini Imerese Prosecutor's Office, which has been investigating the tragedy , points the finger at the crew of the Bayesian and not some suspected design flaw. Three crew members, including the captain, are under investigation for the incident that claimed the lives of seven people on August 19, 2024, just off the port of Porticello, Italy.\nWhile a previous report by the UK's Marine Accident Investigation Branch (MAIB) noted that a design flaw of the Bayesian was largely responsible for the sinking, the Prosecutor's Office says it as the crew's experience, and ultimately negligence, that's to blame. The full report has not yet been submitted, but a synopsis has been provided to the local media .\nAccording to the findings, the crew should have had the keel of the Bayesian fully extended, allowing it to bounce back after listing. Warnings for bad weather on that night had been sent out in advance, so the sudden gusts of wind that broke out at past 3 o'clock in the morning should not have taken the crew by surprise, finding them unprepared.\nPhoto: Perini Navi/ Danny Wheelz Because the keel wasn't fully extended, when one gust of wind hit the Bayesian sideways at speeds of 70 knots (80.5 mph/129.5 kph), the vessel listed and wasn't capable of returning to an even keel. Instead, it began to take on water and, in just 27 minutes, it had sunk completely.\nPrior to the findings , reports claimed that a design flaw must have caused the incident, specifically its record-setting mast. Bayesian was a luxury sailing yacht built by Perini Navi in 2008, equipped with the tallest aluminum mast in the world. That 75-meter (246-foot) mast was believed to have dragged the ship down when it listed, working against its in-built ability to bounce back to an even keel.\nThe Bayesian was raised from the seabed in June 2025, but only after the mast was separated from the vessel. In the spring of 2026, The Italian Sea Group (TISG), which owns the Perini Navi brand, Admiral, and Tecnomar, announced it would be filing for bankruptcy, with an auction for all brands scheduled to take place later this month.\nPhoto: Perini Navi Bayesian was owned by British billionaire Mike Lynch, who died in the incident together with his 18-year-old daughter. The ship was launched in 2008 as Salute and, because of its characteristics and that record-setting mast, soon became a legend. Perini Navi themselves described it more than once as \"unsinkable.\"\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBayesian Perini Navi TISG Sailing yacht luxury yacht accident Italy\nAbout the author: Elena Gorgan\nElena has been writing for a living since 2006 and, as a journalist, she has put her double major in English and Spanish to good use. She covers automotive and mobility topics like cars and bicycles, and she always knows the shows worth watching on Netflix and friends. Full profile\nUP NEXT Keyvany Porroo Looks Like a Million-Dollar Ferrari Transformer in Dubai",
